        paragraphs: [
          "Every pitched note is a vibration that repeats. The repetition rate is the fundamental frequency, measured in hertz (Hz): the A above middle C is tuned to 440 Hz, and each octave up doubles the frequency [1][2]. A plucked string or a buzzing pair of lips almost never vibrates at just one frequency, though. It also vibrates at whole-number multiples of the fundamental (2×, 3×, 4×…), the harmonics or overtones, and these blend into a single perceived pitch [1][2].",
          "Why does a C on a saxophone sound different from the same C on a trumpet or a piano? Mostly because of three things. The first is the spectrum, meaning how strong each harmonic is and where the instrument's resonances (formants) boost particular frequency regions. The second is the envelope, meaning how the sound starts, holds and dies away. The third is noise: breath, bow hiss, hammer thump, key clicks. Perceptual studies of 'timbre space' keep finding the same main dimensions: attack time, spectral centroid (how much energy sits high up, which we hear as 'brightness'), and how the spectrum changes over time [3][4]. Players change all three from moment to moment, which is a big part of what jazz musicians mean by a personal sound.",
          "Young, healthy ears hear from about 20 Hz to 20 kHz, but not evenly. Equal-loudness contours (ISO 226:2003) show that a quiet 50 Hz tone needs far more acoustic energy than a 3 kHz tone to sound equally loud. Our sensitivity peaks at roughly 2–5 kHz, partly because the ear canal resonates there [5][6][7]. That is why the 'edge' of a trumpet or a singer's upper harmonics carries over a band, and why bass instruments need so much more power to be heard.",
          "Pitch is not simply the lowest frequency present. If a sound's harmonics are 200, 300, 400 and 500 Hz, we hear a 100 Hz pitch even when no energy at 100 Hz exists. This is the 'missing fundamental', and the brain infers pitch from the regular spacing of the harmonics [8]. It is why a double bass, whose small body radiates its lowest fundamentals weakly, still sounds clearly pitched, and why a bass line survives on a phone speaker that cannot reproduce 41 Hz at all [8][1].",
        ],
